; P1: the routing core, ported from tests/test_router.py as the first
; differential proof. The suite needs no transport and no loop — the router is
; pure — and lib/dune gives the library no [libraries], so this is the whole
; dependency story.
(test
 (name test_router)
 (libraries seekwright alcotest))

; The port of tests/test_router.py's stdlib-only case, as a dependency fact.
; Two checks, and each catches what the other cannot: a reference to
; [Mcp_caps] or any other out-of-library library fails the compile with
; "Unbound module"; and [ocamldep] run with [-I ../lib] resolves references
; *inside* the library, so a reference from the router to a sibling module
; adds a dependency line here and fails this diff. [-I] is load-bearing: an
; ocamldep with no include path cannot resolve a sibling reference at all,
; which left every cross-module line empty and the pin vacuous until P5.
; External packages ([Yojson], [Uri], [Eio] …) resolve to nothing here, so
; only the library's own modules appear. The pin lists every file the library
; has, so a new module re-pins two lines even when the router ignores it: that
; is deliberate, it is what makes a router that reaches for a sibling visible.
(rule
 (targets router_dependencies.actual)
 (deps (glob_files ../lib/*.ml) (glob_files ../lib/*.mli))
 (action
  (with-outputs-to router_dependencies.actual
   (run ocamldep -I ../lib %{deps}))))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action (diff router_dependencies.expected router_dependencies.actual)))

